{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SMXCAKK153Q6ADBh7tGR85","type":"tool_result","content":"---\nname: 495-lire-le-journal-d-une-campagne\ndescription: \"How to read a 495 campaign dossier directly from its SQLite ledger and CAS, without Pi\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 498a55b5-f197-4c74-acb9-a7271d394176\n  modified: 2026-09-23T08:19:19.911Z\n---\n\nA 495 campaign dossier under `~/.495-campagnes/<nom>/` is readable without Pi, and this is the only\nway to inspect one. Established on 2026-09-19 while instructing the Flash-Next campaign.\n\nSince 2026-09-22 one script does part of it: `node scripts/compare-dossiers.ts --local <dir>\n--distant <dir>` reads two dossiers back read-only and says whether the same contract case was\nrendered on both — the outcome, gates, reference, changed paths and control verdicts decide and set\nthe exit code; interventions, tool calls, durations, tokens, schema refusals, context rewrites and\nfile contents are printed but held against neither side. It answers \"do these two agree\", not \"what\nhappened in this one\"; for that, read the ledger directly as below.\n\nSince 2026-09-23 a second one, `node scripts/measure-budgets.ts <dir>`, reads one dossier: per\nintervention its calls, duration, rate, tokens and cost (from `intervention.finished.cost`, \"non\ninscrit\" when a dossier predates the field), and which bound would fall first at that rate. Its\nbounds come from `<dir>/config.json` *as it stands now* — the ledger never records the budgets a\ncampaign ran under. Both scripts open the change through `scripts/lib/dossier.ts`.\n\nOpen `state.sqlite` with `new DatabaseSync(path, { readOnly: true })` from `node:sqlite` (Node 24,\nno dependency). Tables: `events` (the chained log, one row per `sequence`, `payload` is JSON),\n`changes` (one row whose `state` column is the whole projected `ChangeState` as JSON — phase,\nstatus, `stop_reason`, `budgets`, `gates`, `attempts`, `interventions`, `open_questions`),\n`artifacts` (metadata only), `evidence`, `decision_requests`, `human_decisions`, `pi_bindings`\n(binds a session to a target by `cwd`), `aggregates`, `programs`, `operations`, `leases`.\n\nAn artifact's **content is not in the database**: `artifacts.content_digest` addresses a file at\n`objects/<alg>/<2 premiers caractères>/<reste>`, plain JSON. That is how the adopted mandate,\nrequirements, protocol, design and candidate manifest are read.\n\nUseful event types to filter on, since `budget.consumed` floods everything else:\n`intervention.started` / `.finished`, `question.opened` / `.answered`, `decision.requested` /\n`.recorded`, `artifact.proposed` / `.adopted`, `gate.decided`, `phase.entered`, `candidate.frozen`,\n`evidence.recorded`, `operation.retried`, `status.changed`.\n\n**Raw model text survives only in a refusal.** An accepted report keeps its parsed form in the\n`output` artifact and nothing else; a report the schema refused keeps `terminal.output.raw`, the\nmodel's text verbatim. So a dossier that completed cannot tell you how the model worded anything,\nand a parser defect is replayable only against a dossier that refused. Proved on 2026-09-22 by\nreplaying `e23-local-rapport-refuse` against two versions of the bare-JSON fallback.\n\nPolling this ledger read-only from a background loop is how a live campaign is followed from\noutside Pi; the writer process is unaffected.\n\n**Why:** every finding of that session — a human decision recorded and never reaching the adopted\nrequirements, the residual-risk lines that belong to qualification witnesses, the real durations of\neach control — came from reading the ledger, and none of it is visible from the TUI or from\n`/495 report`.\n\n**How to apply:** reuse when instructing a chantier that cites a campaign, or when following a run.\nSee [[495-environment-facts]] for where the campaigns live.\n---\nname: 495-recette-e10s02-reperes\ndescription: \"Recette e10s02 (2026-10-03) — banc ~/.495-campagnes/scripts/e10s02-*, deux défauts antérieurs vus en chemin (pmd sur un réacteur à dépendance entre modules ; campagne npm qui compte un candidat raté comme défaut du harnais)\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 80ba87d1-e931-4c08-8202-22583d75398b\n  modified: 2026-10-03T04:04:16.580Z\n---\n\nRecette de e10s02 à 3a759ac, le 2026-10-03, rendue `prete`. Banc : `e10s02-preparer.mjs` (cibles genere, seul-genere,\nfausse-marque, reacteur, reacteur-indep), `e10s02-run.sh` (reprend `e10s01-drive.ts` et `e10s01-agent-qualite.json`),\n`e10s02-lire.mjs`. Builds : `builds/e10s02-tete-3a759ac`, `builds/e10s02-main-32cb694`. Le rapport sort en anglais\nseulement avec `HARNESS495_LANGUAGE=en`, la langue de la question n'y change rien.\n\nDeux défauts antérieurs à la branche, que ni le registre ni la branche ne nomment au 2026-10-03 :\n- **pmd sur un réacteur dont un module dépend d'un autre** : la commande `mvn -o pmd:pmd` est lancée sans phase,\n  Maven 3.9 ne résout pas le module voisin, et G2 tombe en FAIL (capability_missing). Même chose sur `main` (e10s01).\n- **`npm run campagne -- npm` écrit DEFECT pour un candidat raté** : vitest n'écrit aucun LCOV dès qu'un test échoue\n  (coverage INDETERMINATE), et Stryker refuse un test qui lit le texte source (l'instrumentation le rallonge).\n  `cycle/src/verdict-campagne.ts` compte les deux comme des défauts du harnais.\n\n**How to apply:** avant de lire un DEFECT npm comme une régression, regarder le verdict `unit` du candidat et le\nstdout de Stryker. Pour un réacteur Maven de recette PMD, des modules indépendants tant que le premier défaut n'est pas corrigé.\n/Users/jeanjerome/.495-campagnes/e10s02-s1-genere-tete:\nconfig.json\ndigest.txt\ndriver.log\ndriver.out\nexports\nlocks\nlogs\nobjects\nstate.sqlite\nstate.sqlite-shm\nstate.sqlite-wal\nworkspaces\n\n/Users/jeanjerome/.495-campagnes/scripts:\ne01s04-etat.mjs\ne01s05-config.json\ne01s05-drive.ts\ne01